{
  "dom_id": "step-t8683",
  "name": "Introduction",
  "problem": "The **intersection** of two intervals is their set of common values, and is denoted by the intersection symbol $\\cap.$\n\nFor example, the intersection of the two intervals $(-\\infty, 2]$ and $[-1,\\infty)$ is denoted\n\n$$\n(-\\infty, 2] \\cap [-1,\\infty)\n$$\n\nand corresponds to the set of values that are in both intervals $(-\\infty, 2]$ and $[-1,\\infty).$\n\nTo represent the intersection on a number line, we can start by sketching the two intervals separately:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14191341/assets/a0b486ac0f981fc5a0161c5eabba9ec257bd0a03e5f0e27397f6fe8e81eb39a5.png)\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14191341/assets/8a98f83055b49c14ed08fff658ace9527c5134879a66245aff02e446d44a5c87.png)\n\nThe intersection $(-\\infty, 2] \\cap [-1,\\infty)$ represents the values that are in both intervals. So, it can be graphed by finding where the two intervals overlap:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14191341/assets/f7a5126928d43be82d3e4a167b52b34d8ed4ec33e94b991ecb8486caf3e3d4a9.png)\n\nTherefore, the intersection of the intervals is ${\\color{blue}[-1,2]}.$ Symbolically, we write\n\n$$\n{\\color{red}(-\\infty,2]} \\cap {\\color{red}[-1,\\infty)} = {\\color{blue}[-1,2]}.\n$$",
